WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.334 --> 00:00:02.706
- I didn't talk until I
was like 15 years old.

00:00:02.706 --> 00:00:05.277
I was pretty much silent,
which no one believes.

00:00:05.277 --> 00:00:06.944
Being kind to people
and listening to people

00:00:06.944 --> 00:00:08.722
tell you about their
families and their lives.

00:00:08.722 --> 00:00:10.024
People will think you're not funny

00:00:10.024 --> 00:00:11.348
because of the way that you look.

00:00:11.348 --> 00:00:14.288
My favorite artists have
always been the ones that

00:00:14.288 --> 00:00:16.373
stick together with their group.

00:00:16.373 --> 00:00:18.678
That feeling of waiting for
this pregnant woman to leave

00:00:18.678 --> 00:00:20.752
so I can finish writing
my little weird plays

00:00:20.752 --> 00:00:22.486
that I would write is when I was like

00:00:22.486 --> 00:00:23.661
oh I love doing this.

00:00:23.661 --> 00:00:25.285
It felt like you better get your own show

00:00:25.285 --> 00:00:26.750
because that's what
everyone else is doing.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:29.404
If anyone should be doing
it right, it should be me.

00:00:29.404 --> 00:00:32.183
And that makes sense and
I actually like that.

00:00:32.183 --> 00:00:34.347
Like I've never been just
like one live for the moment

00:00:34.347 --> 00:00:36.223
and who cares what
happened like c'est la vie

00:00:36.223 --> 00:00:37.615
like I was never that ...

00:00:37.615 --> 00:00:39.063
I was highly organized.

00:00:39.063 --> 00:00:40.911
I just want to be skinny
since I was 12 years old.

00:00:40.911 --> 00:00:42.968
I've just been like, oh god I just wish

00:00:42.968 --> 00:00:44.419
I could be one of the skinny girls.

00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:45.282
Never happened.

00:00:45.282 --> 00:00:49.642
I meet handsome men in cute
and unusual ways on television

00:00:49.642 --> 00:00:52.326
and next season my character
might get a pet puppy.

00:00:52.326 --> 00:00:54.957
- She's an American actress,
comedian and writer.

00:00:54.957 --> 00:00:57.100
She's the creator and
star of the Fox sitcom

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.414
"The Mindy Project".

00:00:58.414 --> 00:01:00.647
She's also known for her
work on the NBC sitcom

00:01:00.647 --> 00:01:01.720
"The Office".

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:02.911
She's Mindy Kaling

00:01:02.911 --> 00:01:06.494
and here are her top
ten rules for success.

00:01:08.105 --> 00:01:10.730
- It is very important to be expressive

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:13.482
in so much that no one
should ever be stopping you

00:01:13.482 --> 00:01:15.522
from saying what you need to say

00:01:15.522 --> 00:01:17.068
or you should ever have that fear.

00:01:17.068 --> 00:01:19.646
However, I think as a young person,

00:01:19.646 --> 00:01:22.090
it's equally important,
if not more important,

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:25.322
to be perceptive and to be sensitive.

00:01:25.322 --> 00:01:27.836
And I feel like we don't
talk about that a lot

00:01:27.836 --> 00:01:30.168
because we feel like the only way

00:01:30.168 --> 00:01:32.328
to show that we're
empowered is by speaking it

00:01:32.328 --> 00:01:35.883
and I think that, you know,
when I was growing up,

00:01:35.883 --> 00:01:39.643
I really thought that what
my parents taught me ...

00:01:39.643 --> 00:01:41.988
I didn't talk until I
was like 15 years old.

00:01:41.988 --> 00:01:44.193
I was pretty much silent,
which no one believes

00:01:44.193 --> 00:01:47.413
and it's because I just was like listening

00:01:47.413 --> 00:01:50.050
and paying attention to
what everyone was saying

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:53.146
and trying to have a good social IQ,

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:55.549
which I think helped me a really,

00:01:55.549 --> 00:01:57.635
which helped me later
to become a good writer

00:01:57.635 --> 00:02:01.031
and I think that that is
the one greatest thing

00:02:01.031 --> 00:02:04.531
is to really, you know,
to listen to others

00:02:04.531 --> 00:02:06.843
more than necessarily
putting such a premium

00:02:06.843 --> 00:02:08.944
on being able to say what you feel.

00:02:08.944 --> 00:02:12.843
The best advice I ever got
is probably from Greg Daniels

00:02:12.843 --> 00:02:15.518
and he's given me tons of good advice

00:02:15.518 --> 00:02:17.784
but one piece of advice he gave me

00:02:17.784 --> 00:02:20.236
which seems very simple
but is very hard to follow

00:02:20.236 --> 00:02:22.304
all the time is be kind.

00:02:22.304 --> 00:02:26.304
If you're in a room with
kind of a bunch of like

00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:30.191
sardonic, East coast educated,

00:02:30.191 --> 00:02:32.084
kind of impatient writers

00:02:32.084 --> 00:02:34.697
it's very hard to show kindness

00:02:34.697 --> 00:02:37.427
and I think that especially
graduating from comedy writer

00:02:37.427 --> 00:02:41.039
to running a show where
you, like I'm a manager,

00:02:41.039 --> 00:02:44.114
I can be sued for like
human resources issues,

00:02:44.114 --> 00:02:47.613
like that's not something
that I came into this doing.

00:02:47.613 --> 00:02:49.382
Being kind to people
and listening to people

00:02:49.382 --> 00:02:51.463
tell you about their
families and their lives

00:02:51.463 --> 00:02:53.982
it's like I think that was a
very useful piece of advice.

00:02:53.982 --> 00:02:57.523
I came up with people who wanted to ...

00:02:57.523 --> 00:03:01.449
Like I said the writers I work
with identify as feminists,

00:03:01.449 --> 00:03:03.690
they want to include women,

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:07.135
they're excited about the
voices of an Indian woman,

00:03:07.135 --> 00:03:08.673
even if they are white men.

00:03:08.673 --> 00:03:12.340
They way that I am also
excited about female voices

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:15.287
in my writing staff
and stand-up comedians,

00:03:15.287 --> 00:03:17.951
but it is been challenging.

00:03:17.951 --> 00:03:20.205
I think one of the things
that's been helpful to me,

00:03:20.205 --> 00:03:23.205
is that I try not to think that much

00:03:25.084 --> 00:03:27.936
about the fact that
I'm an Indian-American.

00:03:27.936 --> 00:03:29.713
So many people will look at me

00:03:29.713 --> 00:03:31.112
and be like you're different
than everyone else.

00:03:31.112 --> 00:03:32.908
But I can't sort of think about it.

00:03:32.908 --> 00:03:34.950
I can't like think about
my legacy or whatever

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.886
because it stops me from being productive.

00:03:36.886 --> 00:03:38.366
It's a distraction.

00:03:38.366 --> 00:03:41.133
And I don't want to deny who I am

00:03:41.133 --> 00:03:43.810
cause it's so important to me

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:45.766
but I can't rely on it.
- Right.

00:03:45.766 --> 00:03:48.841
And that's just been something
that I think is so important

00:03:48.841 --> 00:03:52.085
because people will try
to make you feel like ...

00:03:52.085 --> 00:03:54.521
I felt that constantly.

00:03:54.521 --> 00:03:56.697
People assuming I'm not funny in New York

00:03:56.697 --> 00:03:58.382
or coming up as a stand-up

00:03:58.382 --> 00:03:59.994
or coming up and doing improv and stuff.

00:03:59.994 --> 00:04:01.473
People will think you're not funny

00:04:01.473 --> 00:04:02.930
because of the way that you look.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:04.189
So you have to just be like

00:04:04.189 --> 00:04:06.751
I'm not going to think
in these terms at all.

00:04:06.751 --> 00:04:08.554
I'm just going to ignore it.
- Right.

00:04:08.554 --> 00:04:10.412
And I think that's my advice to people

00:04:10.412 --> 00:04:12.705
is like you can get so
hung up on being like

00:04:12.705 --> 00:04:14.154
am I the victim of this situation?

00:04:14.154 --> 00:04:16.821
And be like I'm in charge of it.

00:04:18.005 --> 00:04:21.423
I think that's been why I've
been able to be successful

00:04:21.423 --> 00:04:25.006
my singular crazy
Richard Nixon-like focus.

00:04:26.091 --> 00:04:27.899
(laughing)
- Cool president to pick.

00:04:27.899 --> 00:04:29.056
- Cool. Cool.

00:04:29.056 --> 00:04:30.228
- He was focused!

00:04:30.228 --> 00:04:32.119
- He was focused.
(laughing)

00:04:32.119 --> 00:04:34.505
My favorite artists have
always been the ones

00:04:34.505 --> 00:04:37.441
that stick together with their group.

00:04:37.441 --> 00:04:40.789
Whether it's Christopher
Guest and his actors

00:04:40.789 --> 00:04:42.036
who are often in a lot.

00:04:42.036 --> 00:04:43.461
He seems like he'd have a company.

00:04:43.461 --> 00:04:45.707
Or Woody Allen using Dianne
Wiest and Mia Farrow.

00:04:45.707 --> 00:04:49.542
And it also just makes the
quality of life so much better

00:04:49.542 --> 00:04:53.327
because my hours are so
long and if I can just pack

00:04:53.327 --> 00:04:55.974
the scenes and the writer's
room with my old friends,

00:04:55.974 --> 00:04:58.006
first of all, things go faster

00:04:58.006 --> 00:05:00.621
and second of all, you get to transition

00:05:00.621 --> 00:05:04.441
from like work to social
life much more easily.

00:05:04.441 --> 00:05:07.920
Now that said all of my
original friends at "The Office"

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:09.668
like Mike Schur, he went on to create

00:05:09.668 --> 00:05:11.418
"Brooklyn" and "Parks", he's very busy.

00:05:11.418 --> 00:05:12.829
Paul is running "Newsroom",

00:05:12.829 --> 00:05:14.953
B.J. has his second book out

00:05:14.953 --> 00:05:17.020
and Greg is, you know, Greg,

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:20.422
but it's great when they can
just come in and do that.

00:05:20.422 --> 00:05:23.685
Also, the tone of The
Office is just great.

00:05:23.685 --> 00:05:25.237
I know that it's a mock documentary

00:05:25.237 --> 00:05:27.588
and our show is very cinematic
and it's very different

00:05:27.588 --> 00:05:31.589
but in terms of the
types of jokes we write,

00:05:31.589 --> 00:05:33.411
that is exactly the same I think.

00:05:33.411 --> 00:05:34.911
I as a little kid,

00:05:36.013 --> 00:05:38.596
my very very protective parents

00:05:39.454 --> 00:05:43.324
who would not let me out a
lot to do a lot of stuff,

00:05:43.324 --> 00:05:46.151
I didn't do any sports, I
was not good at instruments,

00:05:46.151 --> 00:05:47.887
we never really traveled or anything,

00:05:47.887 --> 00:05:50.246
I was just like told to
study both my parents worked,

00:05:50.246 --> 00:05:51.781
study and read books.

00:05:51.781 --> 00:05:54.866
And so I think that so
much of my childhood

00:05:54.866 --> 00:05:58.115
was sitting, my mom was a
doctor and she'd have her office

00:05:58.115 --> 00:06:00.258
where she would see
patients and talk to them

00:06:00.258 --> 00:06:02.118
and then the exam room and
there was an extra room

00:06:02.118 --> 00:06:04.302
that was for storage and for phlebotomy,

00:06:04.302 --> 00:06:06.719
which is like drawing bloods,

00:06:07.679 --> 00:06:10.302
my mom didn't want to leave
me with a nanny after school,

00:06:10.302 --> 00:06:12.195
I would take the bus to her office,

00:06:12.195 --> 00:06:14.661
go to this last room and I would sit there

00:06:14.661 --> 00:06:16.640
with a typewriter and I
would either read or write,

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:18.104
that was all I could do.

00:06:18.104 --> 00:06:19.206
And then if someone had to come in

00:06:19.206 --> 00:06:20.457
and take their bloods taken,

00:06:20.457 --> 00:06:22.567
I would just get shuffled out.
(audience laughing)

00:06:22.567 --> 00:06:24.220
And then a pregnant woman
would just be sitting there

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:25.322
getting her bloods drawn

00:06:25.322 --> 00:06:26.844
and then I would kind of wait impatiently,

00:06:26.844 --> 00:06:29.000
and then she's leave and I'd come back in

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:30.397
and start writing again,

00:06:30.397 --> 00:06:31.855
to the point where they would
like read what I was writing

00:06:31.855 --> 00:06:33.598
and I would write these stories

00:06:33.598 --> 00:06:36.233
and I think that that
feeling of like waiting

00:06:36.233 --> 00:06:38.469
for this pregnant woman to
leave so I could finish writing

00:06:38.469 --> 00:06:40.242
my little weird plays that I would write

00:06:40.242 --> 00:06:41.935
is when I was like oh I love doing this.

00:06:41.935 --> 00:06:45.141
And I can do it without
anybody else to entertain me.

00:06:45.141 --> 00:06:49.215
- To have a successful pilot
on network television right now

00:06:49.215 --> 00:06:52.965
is so rare, you must
have been over the moon.

00:06:54.304 --> 00:06:55.663
How did it happen?

00:06:55.663 --> 00:06:58.352
What was the secret to your success?

00:06:58.352 --> 00:07:01.565
- Well I think that I had been ...

00:07:01.565 --> 00:07:04.284
It is very rare and it's ...

00:07:04.284 --> 00:07:06.979
I was so lucky and I've had so many people

00:07:06.979 --> 00:07:10.503
involved from all of our
studio execs and Bela Bajaria

00:07:10.503 --> 00:07:12.898
and everyone at Fox and Kevin Riley

00:07:12.898 --> 00:07:14.817
who I had a long relationship with

00:07:14.817 --> 00:07:17.435
but Greg Daniels had a writer's room

00:07:17.435 --> 00:07:19.320
in season two of "The Office"

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:22.056
it was like Gene
Stupnitsky, Lee Eisenberg,

00:07:22.056 --> 00:07:24.350
who then went on to start "Trophy Wife"

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:25.531
and write "Bad Teacher".

00:07:25.531 --> 00:07:27.700
B.J. Novak whose done
a ton of amazing stuff.

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:29.861
Mike Schur who did "Parks and
Rec" and then now whatever

00:07:29.861 --> 00:07:32.578
Paul Lieberstein is now
show writing "The Newsroom"

00:07:32.578 --> 00:07:33.971
and does director episodes,

00:07:33.971 --> 00:07:36.764
so of that group it's not that
weird to have your own show

00:07:36.764 --> 00:07:37.597
- Right.

00:07:38.972 --> 00:07:42.491
- We're very competitive and
sort of high-achieving people

00:07:42.491 --> 00:07:44.795
in the season two writer's room.

00:07:44.795 --> 00:07:48.168
So it felt like you
better get your own show

00:07:48.168 --> 00:07:50.012
because that's what
everyone else is doing,

00:07:50.012 --> 00:07:52.118
which is a good atmosphere
to be in, I think.

00:07:52.118 --> 00:07:55.250
- Diversity has become
a thing with this show,

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:57.163
the topic of contention.

00:07:57.163 --> 00:08:01.089
But I'm more interested in
knowing how you feel about that

00:08:01.089 --> 00:08:03.997
in terms of having to address it

00:08:03.997 --> 00:08:06.358
and justify your decisions.

00:08:06.358 --> 00:08:07.775
Has it been ...

00:08:07.775 --> 00:08:09.817
- You know, it's so important to me

00:08:09.817 --> 00:08:13.064
so I feel a lot of times when
I talk about it that I get

00:08:13.064 --> 00:08:16.206
really passionate about it.
- Passionate. Yeah.

00:08:16.206 --> 00:08:17.544
And I ultimately take it as a compliment

00:08:17.544 --> 00:08:19.756
cause I just get held
up to a higher standard

00:08:19.756 --> 00:08:22.116
because there's a sense that

00:08:22.116 --> 00:08:24.957
if anyone should be doing
it right, it should be me.

00:08:24.957 --> 00:08:27.776
And that makes sense,
and I actually like that.

00:08:27.776 --> 00:08:30.162
I like that people have a
higher expectation of me

00:08:30.162 --> 00:08:31.579
than other shows.

00:08:32.594 --> 00:08:36.572
I think we can always be doing better.

00:08:36.572 --> 00:08:38.587
Sometimes what people
don't see behind the scenes

00:08:38.587 --> 00:08:41.892
is like we want to get
Kevin Hart on the show

00:08:41.892 --> 00:08:44.176
or Dulay and they can't
do it cause of scheduling.

00:08:44.176 --> 00:08:48.640
People don't know that
cause we don't advertise it.

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:49.857
I love our cast.

00:08:49.857 --> 00:08:51.702
I like Xosha is so funny and so great.

00:08:51.702 --> 00:08:54.463
I think too, dark skinned women of color

00:08:54.463 --> 00:08:57.755
as series regulars in a show,
one of them as the lead,

00:08:57.755 --> 00:08:59.087
I feel really proud of that.

00:08:59.087 --> 00:09:02.907
And that said, we are
always trying to do better.

00:09:02.907 --> 00:09:05.214
- Do you have a blueprint for your career?

00:09:05.214 --> 00:09:09.262
Not a blueprint cleanse
but an actual blueprint.

00:09:09.262 --> 00:09:11.802
- Yeah, I've always been
one of those weird kids

00:09:11.802 --> 00:09:15.885
when I would go to high
school I would make lists

00:09:17.125 --> 00:09:19.359
of all the things I wanted to do

00:09:19.359 --> 00:09:22.576
and kind of check them off,
like a serial murderer,

00:09:22.576 --> 00:09:24.544
like did I do this, and
do this, and do this.

00:09:24.544 --> 00:09:26.908
And when I went to college
I assessed what I thought

00:09:26.908 --> 00:09:29.760
was cool to accomplish
and then I checked it off.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:32.051
Like I've never been just
one live for the moment,

00:09:32.051 --> 00:09:33.830
and who cares what
happened, like c'est la vie,

00:09:33.830 --> 00:09:35.905
I was never that, I was highly organized.

00:09:35.905 --> 00:09:38.738
And so I think when I was younger,

00:09:40.157 --> 00:09:42.535
this is one of the cooler
things about my life

00:09:42.535 --> 00:09:44.370
and I have to acknowledge it sometimes

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:46.961
cause when I get mad at it
being humid or whatever,

00:09:46.961 --> 00:09:49.487
that I was, I am living my life's dream.

00:09:49.487 --> 00:09:52.381
Like the job I had now, I
had a plan up until now.

00:09:52.381 --> 00:09:54.318
Which was to get my own television show

00:09:54.318 --> 00:09:55.861
that I would create.

00:09:55.861 --> 00:10:00.185
And so I have to start
thinking more, unless I die.

00:10:00.185 --> 00:10:02.041
(audience laughing)
So I could die.

00:10:02.041 --> 00:10:04.224
I could die this week
or something that would

00:10:04.224 --> 00:10:07.868
be pretty dramatic and cool
(audience laughing)

00:10:07.868 --> 00:10:12.188
But I borrowing that I have
to make some more plans.

00:10:12.188 --> 00:10:13.206
'Cause now I'm looking inward, I'm like

00:10:13.206 --> 00:10:15.260
oh I should maybe have kids, get married,

00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:16.815
maybe that sort of thing,
I should do that now.

00:10:16.815 --> 00:10:19.563
What I'm interested in and
when I'm telling stories

00:10:19.563 --> 00:10:22.377
is that you think you want
one thing your whole life.

00:10:22.377 --> 00:10:24.782
For me, my whole life, I
just want to be skinny.

00:10:24.782 --> 00:10:27.089
Since I was 12 years
old, I've just been like

00:10:27.089 --> 00:10:29.230
oh god I just wish I could
be one of the skinny girls.

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:30.398
Never happened.

00:10:30.398 --> 00:10:33.374
And I wish I had thick, beautiful
hair and everything else

00:10:33.374 --> 00:10:34.745
and for this character, what's been fun

00:10:34.745 --> 00:10:36.713
is she just wanted to get
married and now it's happening

00:10:36.713 --> 00:10:40.184
and she's like I want more.
(agreeing)

00:10:40.184 --> 00:10:42.666
And that is something
that is super relatable

00:10:42.666 --> 00:10:44.598
and it's the one thing that seems

00:10:44.598 --> 00:10:46.359
when no matter who I meet whose a woman

00:10:46.359 --> 00:10:47.903
that seems very constant.

00:10:47.903 --> 00:10:49.479
So I like telling that story.

00:10:49.479 --> 00:10:51.956
So even though I have no idea

00:10:51.956 --> 00:10:54.215
why I was asked to speak here today,

00:10:54.215 --> 00:10:56.682
I prepared this speech very carefully

00:10:56.682 --> 00:10:59.808
the way that any Dartmouth
educated graduate would.

00:10:59.808 --> 00:11:02.606
I drank a 40 of jägermeister
(audience laughing)

00:11:02.606 --> 00:11:05.391
then I called my dad to see
if he would get me out of it

00:11:05.391 --> 00:11:07.771
he's here today, he could
not get me out of it.

00:11:07.771 --> 00:11:10.686
So I tried to hire a college
freshman to write it for me

00:11:10.686 --> 00:11:14.382
in exchange for a $200 gift
card to Newbury Comics.

00:11:14.382 --> 00:11:15.682
That didn't work out.

00:11:15.682 --> 00:11:19.054
Finally, seeing that I
absolutely had to do this

00:11:19.054 --> 00:11:20.570
and couldn't get out of it,

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:23.494
rolled up my sleeves,
sat down at my computer,

00:11:23.494 --> 00:11:25.782
and tried to buy a
commencement address off of

00:11:25.782 --> 00:11:27.881
movingcommencementspeeches.com.

00:11:27.881 --> 00:11:29.949
My credit card was declined

00:11:29.949 --> 00:11:33.268
so I had to write the thing
myself and here we are today.

00:11:33.268 --> 00:11:35.860
There are many, many
distinguished speakers

00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:37.073
who have spoken here today.

00:11:37.073 --> 00:11:39.476
I am sharing the stage with Preet Bharara,

00:11:39.476 --> 00:11:42.085
U.S. Attorney for the
Southern District of New York,

00:11:42.085 --> 00:11:43.930
you've heard what a great guy he is.

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:46.454
In 2012, he was named by Time magazine

00:11:46.454 --> 00:11:49.362
as one of the 100 Most
Influential People in the World,

00:11:49.362 --> 00:11:51.876
which apparently they're just giving out.

00:11:51.876 --> 00:11:53.425
(audience laughing)

00:11:53.425 --> 00:11:55.601
According to Time, he
has battled terrorism,

00:11:55.601 --> 00:11:58.355
as evidenced by his conviction
of the Times Square bomber.

00:11:58.355 --> 00:12:00.066
He's crippled international arms dealers,

00:12:00.066 --> 00:12:03.928
drug traffickers and dealt
with financial fraud.

00:12:03.928 --> 00:12:07.220
Clearly, Harvard wanted you
to see the full range of

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:10.615
what India can produce here.
(audience laughing)

00:12:10.615 --> 00:12:14.007
Mr. Bharara fights finance
criminals and terrorism,

00:12:14.007 --> 00:12:18.174
I meet handsome men in cute
and unusual ways on television,

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:22.050
and next season my character
might get a pet puppy.

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:24.732
So is one more important than the other?

00:12:24.732 --> 00:12:25.762
Who can say.

00:12:25.762 --> 00:12:28.632
The real reason I am
here is as Connie said,

00:12:28.632 --> 00:12:31.038
I am obsessed with justice.

00:12:31.038 --> 00:12:34.994
Not so much with the law but with justice.

00:12:34.994 --> 00:12:38.660
Actually in my mind, law
is that pesky thing that

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:40.558
often gets in the way of justice.

00:12:40.558 --> 00:12:44.107
I believe in the Clint
Eastwood school of the law.

00:12:44.107 --> 00:12:46.703
An eye for an eye, I don't think so.

00:12:46.703 --> 00:12:48.271
That solves nothing.

00:12:48.271 --> 00:12:52.259
You take my eye, I take
your life my friend, okay?

00:12:52.259 --> 00:12:54.466
In a duel. Aaron Burr style.

00:12:54.466 --> 00:12:56.335
I don't want your stupid eye.

00:12:56.335 --> 00:12:57.797
For what, my eye collection?

00:12:57.797 --> 00:12:59.956
You're dead. Kay?

00:12:59.956 --> 00:13:01.902
Yes, duels are the first
thing that you learn

00:13:01.902 --> 00:13:03.776
when you enter my graduate program.

00:13:03.776 --> 00:13:06.507
The Harvard School of Vengeance.

00:13:06.507 --> 00:13:09.222
But again that is not what I came to here

00:13:09.222 --> 00:13:10.377
to talk to you about.

00:13:10.377 --> 00:13:12.771
That's for the reception after,
we can talk about that more.

00:13:12.771 --> 00:13:14.392
The Harvard Law School crest,

00:13:14.392 --> 00:13:17.504
which you can see in front of you,

00:13:17.504 --> 00:13:20.818
has the word veritas,
which means truth in Latin.

00:13:20.818 --> 00:13:23.667
I know this because though
I have been known as Mindy

00:13:23.667 --> 00:13:26.365
my whole life, my first
name is actually Vera,

00:13:26.365 --> 00:13:28.103
which also means truth.

00:13:28.103 --> 00:13:30.808
It's true, it's actually too
boring to make that fact up.

00:13:30.808 --> 00:13:32.950
And if you look at the
crest, you'll notice

00:13:32.950 --> 00:13:36.283
that under this hallowed word, there are

00:13:37.270 --> 00:13:41.871
three bunches of asparagus.
(audience laughing)

00:13:41.871 --> 00:13:45.954
Because asparagus is the
tallest and the proudest

00:13:46.831 --> 00:13:48.331
of the vegetables.

00:13:49.183 --> 00:13:51.424
The pilar of the vegetable kingdom

00:13:51.424 --> 00:13:53.017
and it's like the Latin ...

00:13:53.017 --> 00:13:55.350
Okay, that is not asparagus.

00:13:56.284 --> 00:13:57.451
That is wheat.

00:13:58.634 --> 00:14:01.648
Which makes also not
a ton of sense either.

00:14:01.648 --> 00:14:05.258
Okay, well that was like
three pages of my speech.

00:14:05.258 --> 00:14:09.088
Nope, that's a callback to asparagus.

00:14:09.088 --> 00:14:11.792
I have this really funny
run about hollandaise.

00:14:11.792 --> 00:14:13.648
You'll never get to hear that.

00:14:13.648 --> 00:14:14.800
You know this isn't going anywhere.

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:16.178
I'm going to move passed trying
to make sense of your crest

00:14:16.178 --> 00:14:17.416
which makes no sense.

00:14:17.416 --> 00:14:18.928
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:14:18.928 --> 00:14:21.439
I made this video because
obinna asked me to.

00:14:21.439 --> 00:14:22.978
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:14:22.978 --> 00:14:24.582
that you want me to profile next,

00:14:24.582 --> 00:14:27.038
leave it in the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:14:27.038 --> 00:14:29.167
I'd also love to know which
of Mindy's top ten rules

00:14:29.167 --> 00:14:31.519
had the biggest impact on you and why.

00:14:31.519 --> 00:14:33.525
Leave it in the comments and
I'll join the discussion.

00:14:33.525 --> 00:14:35.057
Thank you so much for watching.

00:14:35.057 --> 00:14:38.557
Continue to believe and I'll see you soon.

